.TH TRANSITMON 1 "September 2026" "TransitMon 0.1.0" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
transitmon \- live terminal monitor for supported OBD-II data
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B transitmon
.RI [ --port " DEVICE" ]
.RI [ --mock ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B transitmon
opens an OBD-II connection and displays live vehicle data in a curses-based terminal interface.
The program is intended for a Linux host such as a headless Debian Raspberry Pi accessed over SSH.
.PP
TransitMon does not assume that every OBD-II sensor exists on the connected vehicle.
At startup it inspects the commands reported as supported by the active ECU connection and only polls dashboard items present in that supported-command set.
Unsupported or null responses are displayed as unavailable rather than being fabricated.
.PP
Version 0.1 is limited to standard OBD-II data available through the python-OBD backend.
Ford-specific enhanced modules and PIDs are outside the scope of this version.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BI --port " DEVICE"
Use the specified serial device for the OBD adapter, for example
.IR /dev/ttyUSB0 .
If omitted, the backend attempts serial-port auto-detection.
.TP
.B --mock
Use the built-in mock vehicle instead of physical OBD hardware.
This is useful for testing the TUI before an adapter is installed.
.TP
.BR -h , " --help"
Show command-line help and exit.
.SH INTERFACE
TransitMon currently provides two pages.
.TP
.BR 1 " or " F1
Open the dashboard page.
The dashboard contains only common values that the connected vehicle reports as supported.
.TP
.BR 2 " or " F2
Open the supported-command page.
This page lists the OBD commands exposed by the current connection.
.TP
.BR Up , " Down"
Scroll the supported-command list when that page is active.
.TP
.B R
Close and reopen the OBD connection, then refresh the supported-command inventory.
.TP
.B Q
Quit TransitMon.
.SH TERMINAL REQUIREMENTS
TransitMon requires a terminal of at least 60 columns by 12 rows.
A larger terminal allows more data to be shown comfortably.
Because the interface uses curses, a valid
.B TERM
environment variable is required.
.SH DATA DISPLAY
The v0.1 dashboard can display values such as RPM, vehicle speed, coolant temperature, calculated engine load, throttle position, mass-air flow, fuel trims, control-module voltage, intake-air temperature, and fuel level when the connected vehicle reports those commands as supported.
.PP
Speed is normalized to miles per hour and supported temperature values are normalized to degrees Fahrenheit by the shared TransitMon OBD backend.
A value of
.B ---
indicates a null or unavailable response.
.SH EXAMPLES
Connect through a USB adapter on
.IR /dev/ttyUSB0 :
.PP
.nf
transitmon --port /dev/ttyUSB0
.fi
.PP
Allow the backend to auto-detect the serial device:
.PP
.nf
transitmon
.fi
.PP
Run without vehicle hardware:
.PP
.nf
transitmon --mock
.fi
.SH SERIAL PERMISSIONS
On Debian systems, access to USB serial adapters commonly requires membership in the
.B dialout
group.
For example:
.PP
.nf
sudo usermod -aG dialout "$USER"
.fi
.PP
Log out and back in after changing group membership.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Normal exit.
.TP
.B 1
The OBD adapter could not be opened or connected, or another operating-system-level connection error occurred.
.TP
.B 2
Invalid command-line usage reported by the argument parser.
.SH NOTES
The actual set of available sensors is determined by the vehicle and ECU, not by TransitMon.
Use
.BR transitprobe (1)
first when characterizing a new vehicle.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR transitprobe (1)
